export abstract anchor FactoryExecution:
    retries:
        Retry policy applies to step attempts, project-wide by default, with
        order or step overrides inside engineer policy. The default is three
        retries after the first attempt, four attempts total, configurable from
        zero to ten per attempt chain. Job and delivery retries are nested and
        counted separately, and total spend is visible.
        Only confirmed terminal failures count: a session confirmed ended, a
        confirmed worker loss, a non-allowance rate-limit refusal, or an
        idempotent launch failure. A factory-side timeout or lost response is
        in-doubt and goes to discovery. An exhausted allowance is a budget
        blocker and consumes no retry.
        An exhausted allowance before an attempt starts creates no attempt and
        leaves the step ready with a budget blocker. During an attempt it pauses
        dispatch on that route and the adapter requests a graceful stop. A
        confirmed stop makes the attempt interrupted without counting a retry
        and returns the step to ready or blocked until budget or allowance is
        restored. An unconfirmed stop follows unknown-liveness rules. A 429 with
        stale or missing allowance data is treated as throttling with an
        immediate refresh; repeated unexplained refusals escalate.
        Classification is code-owned; adapters report evidence and declared
        class. Attempts store class, evidence, and retry-counted flag. Every
        retry path, including job, delivery, and launch, passes the same
        effect-class gate; the effect key is stable per operation target, not
        per attempt. An at-most-once or lookup-gated result never retries beyond
        what its class permits.
        Backoff is 30 seconds, 2 minutes, 8 minutes, then capped at 10 minutes,
        with 20 percent clamped jitter. A provider Retry-After takes precedence
        even above the cap. Deadlines persist across restart. A backing-off step
        is not ready, reports retry backoff, and holds no capacity slot or next
        reservation until admission.
        Circuit breakers are scoped to account, harness, and model route. Three
        consecutive transient failures within 10 minutes trip a breaker;
        cooldown is 5 minutes, then a half-open probe. Open routes are skipped;
        all routes open is a blocker. Route switching obeys budget admission and
        the paid-fallback decision.
        Replanning is capped at two authorized replans per order or incident
        before engineer approval. It supersedes the old step, migrates edges,
        and creates a fresh chain only through the recorded revision.
        Retries reuse the worktree unless an authorized revision resets it, and
        partial writes are preserved. Retry admission uses the priority-aware
        budget path.
    failedAttempts:
        The factory allows limited automatic retries for confirmed transient
        execution failures, such as a confirmed worker loss or a non-allowance
        rate-limit refusal. An exhausted allowance is a budget blocker and does
        not consume a retry. A lost response or unknown liveness is in-doubt and
        follows discovery rather than retry. Retry limits are configurable.
        Problems with the work itself become help requests through the defined
        escalation path. Failures that exhaust automatic retries also enter the
        defined escalation path.
    priority:
        Priority is an integer from 0 to 100, default 50, higher wins. Named
        defaults are critical 90, high 75, normal 50, and low 25. Steps inherit
        order priority. A planner may lower a step or keep it equal; a step
        cannot outrank its order. Raising above the order requires an order
        revision. Overrides record a reason.
        Dispatch chooses the highest-priority ready and eligible step.
        Eligibility means dependencies satisfied, budget and account allowance
        reserved, and a concurrency slot available. Build and test capacity is
        not part of step eligibility. Ties favor a step with a prior attempt,
        then persisted ready time, then older order by submission time, then
        step identifier. Ready time resets when a step becomes unready and ready
        again.
        Running work and running jobs are never preempted and retain start-time
        priority. Priority changes affect queued work immediately. Reservations
        are taken at dispatch, not enqueue. Step admission is atomic across
        budget, account allowance, and concurrency slot, keyed by route. Build
        and test capacity units are admitted at job admission, which commits a
        job-level sub-reservation atomically from the owning step's budget
        reservation and its capacity lease. A step does not reserve build
        capacity before it has a job. A high-priority step may bypass queued
        lower-priority work but never a committed live attempt.
    jobs:
        A job record carries identifier, kind, serving step, wiring version,
        inputs, required capacity units, priority, state, attempts, timeout,
        cache key, artifacts, logs, result, and timestamps. States are queued,
        running, succeeded, failed, timed_out, cancelled, unknown, and orphaned.
        Admission commits intent, idempotency key, job-level budget
        sub-reservation, capacity lease, and pool selection atomically before
        external dispatch. A lease records pool, units, owner, and fencing
        epoch. Workers heartbeat; an expired lease moves the job to unknown, not
        failed. Recovery reconciles leases and jobs before dispatch.
        If job admission is refused, no external effect occurs. The job remains
        queued with a job-capacity or job-budget blocker; the step stays running
        and holds its concurrency slot and budget reservation until the job is
        admitted or the step is cancelled. A job whose required units exceed
        every pool maximum is unsatisfiable and escalates. A large high-priority
        job may be bypassed by smaller work; priority-inversion reporting is the
        mitigation and no unit reservation is pre-taken.
        Workers and pools declare kinds, capacity units, labels, and
        availability, including draining and offline. Units are additive per
        pool and exclusive to a job; multi-worker jobs declare units per worker.
        The queue orders by priority then ready time and scans past jobs that do
        not fit. Running jobs retain start-time priority.
        Cache keys include commit, command, environment hash, toolchain or image
        digest, dependency lock, test selection, wiring version, and assumption
        generations. Entries are reused only when the wiring check is valid and
        provenance hashes verify. Default lifetime is 7 days. Cross-order reuse
        requires explicit policy. Coalescing shares a job only within allowed
        scope, and cancelling one consumer does not cancel a shared job unless no
        consumers remain.
        Job retries default to one retry after the first attempt, separate from
        step retries. Infrastructure or worker failure is retryable; a failing
        test is a result; a test timeout is a result unless the executor
        confirms infrastructure failure. Attempts carry parent links and
        retry-counted flags.
        One executor contract covers local and remote: launch, status, cancel,
        lookup by idempotency key, artifact and log retrieval, and usage. Local
        execution uses the same contract. Exit codes map to succeeded, failed,
        or infrastructure failure, and wrappers cannot hide infrastructure
        failures. Cancellation follows the cancellation rules; capacity is
        released only after worker confirmation. A cancelled job preserves logs
        and partial artifacts.
    jobRecovery:
        A committed capacity lease survives a crash between admission and
        dispatch. Recovery reconciles admitted-unlaunched, launch-in-gap,
        lease-expired, succeeded-uncommitted, and orphaned jobs before new
        dispatch. A late worker result is accepted only when its fencing epoch
        is current; an older epoch is recorded as orphaned. Unknown jobs have a
        configurable resolution deadline, after which capacity is released, the
        reservation stays a floor, and the incident escalates.
